#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193530} :activity/id #uuid "15829972-de8f-5398-aedc-ee127d53c955" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048304 :topic/id #uuid "92d07a33-6647-5dd0-9be4-64be21e6041d" :topic/math-academy-id 3950} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193827 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to use Riemann sums to approximate definite integrals of *composite* functions. As we'll see, this has some interesting applications.\n\nFor example, suppose $f(x)$ is a positive, continuous function. Some values for $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [1,5]$ are given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nLet's use this data to approximate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo approximate this definite integral, we first define a new function $g(x)$ as\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2\\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nNow, our task is to approximate\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals.\n\nThe (regular) step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5-1}{4} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $\\Delta x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x \\approx (g(1) + g(2) + g(3) + g(4)) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nLet's add a row to our table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $g(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $9$ | $16$ | $50$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{1}^{5}g(x)dx \\\\\n&\\approx (3 + 8 + 9 + 16) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a right Riemann sum." :tutorial/id #uuid "c2502b79-d3d8-53e4-be62-b01752a16be9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6553 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1997ebaf-5bad-5fd7-875b-27650eb3f5b6" :step/math-academy-id 39566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192408 :question/id #uuid "d8b6c55c-c272-5818-a113-40e6218fe12c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "824c497f-a01d-53c5-bcfe-e6cd35e69a88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049386}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193067}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Riemann Sum of a Composite Function"} :step/id #uuid "99cb3798-47dd-5790-b2f7-fdfbcde6185f" :step/math-academy-id 39567 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192409 :question/id #uuid "c32c93a7-189d-5dc5-94a4-943ac5bbf29a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10485"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34555306-3c72-55ef-a59f-ab8d97ae4919" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503677}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Arc Length"} :step/id #uuid "7001e0a2-bc0e-5f22-adfb-6688e2d5c0c7" :step/math-academy-id 39568 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192410 :question/id #uuid "dbd945a6-786d-515e-8ecd-351dcf6b29f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9946876-7435-589a-a18d-c89c3d790754" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050438}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193028}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Riemann Sum Gives an Overestimate or an Underestimate"} :step/id #uuid "d04db767-8034-5d9f-8871-0fb87a4da642" :step/math-academy-id 39569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192420 :question/id #uuid "54c6bc65-515f-52dd-8c37-ef6742464361" :question/math-academy-id "e-10520"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea7448d8-a235-52f4-a475-006943f8a142" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193068}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Volumes of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "b9769694-1018-5682-8dd1-facf2627a6f4" :step/math-academy-id 39706 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193695}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193831 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use Riemann sums to approximate areas under curves of composite functions. Our final task is determining whether these approximations overestimate or underestimate the true areas.\n\nWe start by considering *right* Riemann sums. Recall the following facts:\n\n- If a function is strictly *increasing*, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n- If a function is strictly *decreasing*, the right Riemann sum *underestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x \\in [1,2]$, and differentiable for $x \\in (1,2)$. Now, consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nSuppose we use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve $y = g(x)$ over $x\\in [1,2]$. Does the right Riemann sum overestimate or underestimate the true area?\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have $f'(x) \\gt 0$.\n- Differentiating our expression for $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n$g^{′}(x)|= (x^{2})^{′} \\cdot f(x) + x^{2} \\cdot f^{′}(x); = 2x \\cdot f(x) + x^{2} \\cdot f^{′}(x)$.\nWe now examine the sign of each factor in this expression to determine the sign of $g'(x)$.Since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,2)$, we have\n$2x \\gt 0, \\qquad f(x) \\gt 0, \\qquad x^2 \\gt 0, \\qquad f'(x) \\gt 0$.\nHence, we obtain\n$g'(x) = \\underbrace{2x}_{\\gt 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2}_{> 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} = \\underbrace{2x \\cdot f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2 \\cdot f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} \\gt 0$.\n\nTherefore, since $g'(x) \\gt 0$, the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a left Riemann sum." :tutorial/id #uuid "4017a527-6912-5d2d-a92d-3424c1399d67" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6582 :tutorial/title "Determining Overestimates and Underestimates"} :step/id #uuid "6cc57411-9166-578d-ad5d-18fd5906fee2" :step/math-academy-id 39707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193904 :tutorial/content "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/59/59f9a4f76889bf4323c7bcf7dad2dd5269698d94f294458eafdd4c5da0fa0580.png)\n\nWhen we rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis (called the **axis of revolution**), we get a **solid of revolution,** as shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc2858aac81addf1d5c19cd3836361d42de4ce844117ab4d42960e45a274026d.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the volume $V$ of this solid of revolution is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\left[ f(x) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169b0392-4eed-5202-921f-c379f4264c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7613 :tutorial/title "Volumes of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "5ed00cb2-4d94-5a81-8e1c-488592b9d60c" :step/math-academy-id 44209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193905 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$.\n\n![](images/e7/e7fb3f6e11e0e2b51fc4ecae9827a4e378a7981450775f2b9330057c319688bf.png)\n\nThe length of the curve between these two points is known as the **arc length** of the curve.\n\nIt can be shown that the arc length is given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can use Riemann sums to approximate arc lengths. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c4aa50-1ce8-52f8-aa97-b2bce6c85623" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7614 :tutorial/title "Arc Length"} :step/id #uuid "4da87fab-f669-5510-93d3-55fea59cbf65" :step/math-academy-id 44210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193532}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192870}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079574} :activity/id #uuid "ab14ac62-463d-51ef-a272-f23c5f8c6190" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048601 :topic/id #uuid "ad9b07fe-9198-5d28-975d-6db81bacb439" :topic/math-academy-id 622} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079692 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following graph of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand its tangent line $\\color{orange} L$ at $x=4$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d3fe9e0849b68aab9ca8a030a94c39c9eca151d6d4baace485996549462536b.png)\n\nFrom this graph, we can see that near $x=4$, the tangent line and the function are very close to each other. So, the line $L(x)$ is a good approximation of the function for points close to $x=4$.\n\n![](images/75/753d910064586bf0928723aaf4621e3502cc4df9b08d2f7449b4a3fd7201aed6.png)\n\nHere, the tangent line approximation of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nat the point $a=4$ is defined by\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(4) + f^{′}(4)(x - 4)\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $f(4)$ and $f'(4)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function at $a=4$, we get\n\n$$\nf(4) = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'(4) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(4) + f^{′}(4)(x - 4) \\\\\n&= 2 + \\frac{1}{4}(x - 4) \\\\\n&= 2 + \\frac{1}{4}x - 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e16ed39-c135-56e4-aec5-07c93c013953" :tutorial/math-academy-id 584 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "38cd448d-b99d-597f-a0de-491f627552be" :step/math-academy-id 617534 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078603 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078240 :question/id #uuid "74aa60fe-a85b-5cee-b57c-377216cbf4a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-296"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dbc9791-0e8b-505b-ad1f-f40c730fd9e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049375}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078979}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Correct Tangent Line Approximation From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "1581404a-b81b-5f7a-88ad-95dc52633b97" :step/math-academy-id 617535 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078604 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078239 :question/id #uuid "4bb9f4f9-72db-5acc-8628-25ef5848163a" :question/math-academy-id "e-295"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cff76d55-4647-547b-af6d-959810d2b8e2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503711}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Square Root of a Number Using a Tangent Line Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "7d515f65-44c9-5c9a-a36e-e32e1a300c5f" :step/math-academy-id 617538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078605 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078238 :question/id #uuid "2e44fea3-d390-550d-8174-555d3008b6d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-294"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0899ab8c-7841-5824-b954-76481006bd7e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078978}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Cube Root of a Number Using a Tangent Line Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "b8337b49-fdff-5e66-8879-e13ccf375182" :step/math-academy-id 617541}] :activity/title "Approximating the Roots of a Number Using Local Linearity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078648}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605} :activity/id #uuid "cac9bb11-d48e-5140-9653-cc117881f57c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176968 :tutorial/content "Under what conditions can we be sure that the global extrema of a function exist?\n\nThe **extreme value theorem** answers this question and states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function defined on the closed interval $[a,b],$ then $f(x)$ always has at least one global maximum and one global minimum on $[a,b]$*.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5].\n$$\n\nNotice that this function satisfies the conditions for the extreme value theorem:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ is *continuous* on the interval $x\\in [2,5]$, and\n\n- the interval $[2,5]$ is *closed*.\n\nSince *both* conditions are satisfied, the extreme value theorem guarantees that $f(x)$ must have at least one global maximum and one global minimum.\n\nTo see that this is indeed true, let's sketch the graph of $f(x).$ \n\n![](images/d2/d2ceffc40ead80b03d73004b663b91a942444bd14ce9e1e68fc98ffebbae3423.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the function $f(x)$ has \n\n- a global maximum at $x=5,$ and\n\n- a global minimum at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "300a3bb8-a79f-5387-8c5e-1e403c2df531" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ff3fa5a-39c6-574d-bfa0-7e467bf44083" :step/math-academy-id 12791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175768 :question/id #uuid "59426214-0bb1-5e8b-9e96-0fb84ec6361c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8c5c661d-87d1-5fdd-b4cf-9b1217993d69" :step/math-academy-id 12792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175769 :question/id #uuid "2d31d7a1-bd6a-5794-a4af-b6cdad4dc64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341981}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "3001b2d1-c302-5b15-b3a6-3ff0fa36821e" :step/math-academy-id 12794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175770 :question/id #uuid "1d90dec2-3c79-5069-ba1b-7e7e3f16ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7889a404-286a-5541-b477-ed0966b909de" :step/math-academy-id 12795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177036 :tutorial/content "We can only apply the extreme value theorem to *continuous* functions defined on *closed* intervals. Both conditions are crucial.\n\nTo see why this is the case, consider the function $g(x),$ given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=(x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5).\n$$\n\nNotice that $g(x)$ is defined on a semi-open interval (the right endpoint is open). As a result, we cannot guarantee that $g(x)$ has a global maximum and a global minimum.\n\n![](images/a7/a7648d89fc02372f8d48a649425fa5dba4c8a86e12e9fa7bbddc69ffe52575c3.png)\n\nSimilarly, consider the function $h(x),$ given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}, & \\,2\\le x<3, \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+1, & \\,3\\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ is defined on the closed interval $[2,5].$ However, since $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[2,5],$ we cannot guarantee that $h(x)$ has both a global maximum and minimum on $[2,5].$\n\n![](images/b1/b1bdc0a3c384e5918bdac46ce5db31c0836069eb8a3383febf2aac86654929ea.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "08bf8574-c1f7-5521-983b-282f7b0c3cc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5745 :tutorial/title "Conditions for Applying the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fa2543e5-fe3a-57fe-a964-118b017f51bc" :step/math-academy-id 35242 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176121}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097447} :activity/id #uuid "cb0bf575-ae5d-5289-83a0-5fe4af2c7572" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048862 :topic/id #uuid "c7162eba-3656-5b62-8b28-391c01075d02" :topic/math-academy-id 1086} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097910 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to approximate the area $\\mathcal A$ bound by the curve $y= x^2+1$ and the lines $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. We can do this using a left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subintervals, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b25a722d5527db77cd7dd5fb581c0ffed9b9324913d390e2633211360b6a8ab.png)\n\nA better approximation can be found by taking more subintervals. For instance, taking $n=8$ subintervals gives:\n\n![](images/17/17f6c8e458b2e6fbd887728cbbcc7cb64a670598256f76cad69d9e5a9c3cf7b5.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $n,$ our approximation gets better and better. In fact, we can get an *exact* value for the area by taking the limit of the Riemann sum as $n\\to\\infty.$\n\nLet's write down that limit. First, we need to express the left Riemann sum using sigma notation. Using $n$ subintervals between the endpoints $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we express\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx {\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(0+k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({(k\\cdot Δx)}^{2}+1)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({k}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}(\\frac{{k}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we used the fact that $x_k = 0 + k\\Delta x = k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1 - 0}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n,$ as we've seen before.\n\nThe exact area is found by computing the limit as $n\\to\\infty.$ So instead of using an approximation symbol $(\\approx),$ we can now use an equality symbol and write\n\n$$\nA = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThe formula above is a bit clunky, so instead of writing it using limits and sigma notation, we introduce a new notation called the **definite integral**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}= \\int_0^1 (x^2 + 1)\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2+1)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the exact area under the graph of $y = f(x) = x^2+1$ between $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis." :tutorial/id #uuid "10c42cfd-895e-5844-b943-efc5be835821" :tutorial/math-academy-id 759 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dbe8a28f-ae9c-5fce-a185-e8029413c272" :step/math-academy-id 1457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097449}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096431 :question/id #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-405"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319069}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "9c8a3595-093e-5ea9-b728-e20ce01d63e5" :step/math-academy-id 1458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097911 :tutorial/content "In general, for a function $f(x)$ defined on $[a,b],$ the **definite integral** of $f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be defined using the limit of a left  Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nThe limit $x=a$ is referred to as the **lower limit**, whereas $x=b$ is called the **upper limit**." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ef3b0cc-ce87-5a51-9a34-0e45b0a342d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 761 :tutorial/title "General Notation"} :step/id #uuid "b767cb65-6bfb-54c1-b5e9-034fb71e87da" :step/math-academy-id 1461 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097448}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096432 :question/id #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" :question/math-academy-id "e-406"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393257}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "1ed0244b-1489-5e87-9ec9-dd2f6566ad85" :step/math-academy-id 1462 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097451}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096433 :question/id #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-407"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396680}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "dfbce95d-2f31-5252-bde7-c60a98b5fb17" :step/math-academy-id 1464 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097914 :tutorial/content "There's nothing special about using a left Riemann sum to define a definite integral. A definite integral can also be defined as the limit of a right Riemann sum,\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nNothing has changed, except that the index starts from $k=1$ instead of $k=0$, and goes up to $k=n.$ \n\n**Note:** In the limit as $n \\to \\infty,$ the left and right Riemann sums give *the exact same result*!" :tutorial/id #uuid "c1ade2ee-0ed4-5dad-b2fc-a3a517dd3f1d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 764 :tutorial/title "Using a Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "1cf484cc-b168-5f89-a236-0169328a1312" :step/math-academy-id 1466 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096434 :question/id #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" :question/math-academy-id "e-408"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393271}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "0e12f8ef-911e-5e4b-85e0-2c32f12ef0b6" :step/math-academy-id 1467}] :activity/title "Defining Definite Integrals Using Left and Right Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096875}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065206} :activity/id #uuid "4dd7be6e-e9a7-53a7-9076-0bf7c12d09ee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048919 :topic/id #uuid "cc7fb2dc-62dc-56f4-976f-30935824115a" :topic/math-academy-id 344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065396 :tutorial/content "When a function $y=y(x)$ contains complex products, quotients, or radicals, we can use logarithmic differentiation to find the derivative $y'(x)$.\n\nFor example, consider the following function:\n\n$$\ny = x^2 \\cdot 2^x\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find $y'(x)$. To do this, we will use the product rule for logarithms, namely\n\n$$\n\\ln(a b)= \\ln a + \\ln b\n$$\n\nWe differentiate our function by following four steps.\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the product and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{2}) + \\ln (2^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= 2\\ln x + x\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Next, we differentiate both sides of our equation using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2\\ln x + x\\ln 2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 2 \\cdot (\\ln x)^{′} + (x)^{′} \\cdot \\ln (2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} + 1 \\cdot \\ln (2) \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\frac{2}{x} + \\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Then, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(\\frac{2}{x} + \\ln 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for\n\n$$\ny =x^2\\cdot 2^x{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x}(\\frac{2}{x} + \\ln 2) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot 2^{x + 1} + x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1895a7f3-5ba4-5a31-85b3-6a7b050b307c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6088 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "caef414d-4f2a-5504-9b92-39a03d2f3c8f" :step/math-academy-id 37035 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065207}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065207 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064146 :question/id #uuid "c24da7ef-0070-5aae-bade-c82ea56476ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9845"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050375}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064662}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"} :step/id #uuid "b6a516b4-d0c4-5ec7-8f7e-12edd0e85c51" :step/math-academy-id 37036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065210}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064147 :question/id #uuid "7db11c8c-344b-5d87-a8ae-3b006e5bd92b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9846"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050542}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064663}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"} :step/id #uuid "57a60273-bc58-5aff-8dcd-d7139782aa2a" :step/math-academy-id 37037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064148 :question/id #uuid "e0cec84d-96cf-5fed-ba05-a5cfc57841a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9847"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050894}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233441}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"} :step/id #uuid "8850f22e-33ee-545a-8f5e-4dc37bda3b30" :step/math-academy-id 37038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065210 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065397 :tutorial/content "In cases where we wish to differentiate a quotient of functions\n\n$$\ny = f(x) = \\dfrac{g(x)}{h(x)}\n$$\n\nwe can apply the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right) = \\ln a - \\ln b\n$$\n\nFor example, let's compute $y'$, where the function $y$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {e^{x^2}}{(1-2x)^2(x-1)^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{2}(x - 1)^{3})) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln (e^{x^{2}}) - \\ln ((1 - 2x)^{2}) - \\ln ((x - 1)^{3}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= x^{2} - 2\\ln (1 - 2x) - 3\\ln (x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} - 2\\ln (1 - 2x) - 3\\ln (x - 1)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 2x - 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - 2x} \\cdot (-2) - 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= 2x + \\frac{4}{1 - 2x} - \\frac{3}{x - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(2x + \\frac{4}{1 - 2x} - \\frac{3}{x - 1})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute our expression for $y$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{2}(x - 1)^{3})(2x + \\frac{4}{1 - 2x} - \\frac{3}{x - 1}) \\\\\n&= (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{2}(x - 1)^{3})((2x(1 - 2x)(x - 1) + 4(x - 1) - 3(1 - 2x))/((1 - 2x)(x - 1))) \\\\\n&= (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{3}(x - 1)^{4})(2x(1 - 2x)(x - 1) + 4(x - 1) - 3(1 - 2x)) \\\\\n&= (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{3}(x - 1)^{4})(-4x^{3} + 6x^{2} + 8x - 7) \\\\\n&= - (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{3}(x - 1)^{4})(4x^{3} - 6x^{2} - 8x + 7) \\\\\n&= - (e^{x^{2}}(4x^{3} - 6x^{2} - 8x + 7))/((1 - 2x)^{3}(x - 1)^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8cf6e625-669a-5fda-b756-e1ce091eaa93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6090 :tutorial/title "Using Logarithms to Differentiate a Quotient"} :step/id #uuid "2141558f-a6d2-515d-902a-620581d72401" :step/math-academy-id 37041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065208}}] :activity/title "Further Logarithmic Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064435}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079578} :activity/id #uuid "44cc6885-bb82-5b20-bcaf-40d9c657e036" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049111 :topic/id #uuid "db673967-9a93-5184-98c4-d6168a02ca86" :topic/math-academy-id 640} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079668 :tutorial/content "We've learned two methods for approximating the value of the derivative at a point by computing the rate of change between two points a distance of $h$ apart, where $h$ is small.\n\n- The forward difference approximation computes the rate of change between the point $x$ and the point $x+h,$ which is a step of $h$ units forward.\n\n$$\nf'(x)  \\approx \\dfrac{f(x+h)- f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n- The backward difference approximation computes the rate of change between the point $x$ and the point $x-h,$ which is a step of $h$ units backward.\n\n$$\nf'(x)  \\approx \\dfrac{f(x)- f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, we will introduce the **central difference approximation**, also known as the **symmetric difference approximation**. In this approximation, we compute the rate of change between the points $x-h$ and $x+h,$ a distance of $2h$ apart.\n\n$$\nf'(x)  \\approx \\dfrac{f(x+h)- f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n![](images/48/48414a74d6f06fdec0f9b07c2a14f42c3a528b578b3a5f7a61cb4eff93016ac2.png)\n\nUsually, the central difference approximation is a better approximation for $f '(x)$ than the forward difference approximation and the backward difference approximation. This is because for the central difference approximation, $x$ is the midpoint between the two points $x-h$ and $x+h$ that are used in the approximation." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ac17418-b32e-5d12-97ef-d94adc56f308" :tutorial/math-academy-id 372 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a60aa634-136d-5259-b7e2-330e5586dcf6" :step/math-academy-id 695 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078606 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078208 :question/id #uuid "98fdea06-a5ad-5d22-b928-ef893424e447" :question/math-academy-id "e-158"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6803a7be-e0e9-5f19-bd0b-711f49853170" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049934}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345032}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given Data or Tables Using a Central Difference Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "dc49f5fa-24d5-522d-94f8-c7cb90c5dfd6" :step/math-academy-id 699 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078607 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078209 :question/id #uuid "62934fda-d972-50d5-acdb-e1f7b2fd63f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-159"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47fdd234-4281-5a38-aa51-22a8934d48a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317492}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "fa5bcebe-0da6-5538-9ccd-5d348315d13a" :step/math-academy-id 701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078608 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078212 :question/id #uuid "d405fda1-1111-56db-ae34-4c0f257852f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-160"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332879}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "a5d43107-12f5-564b-8b7e-258d9b82d9da" :step/math-academy-id 703}] :activity/title "Estimating Derivatives Using a Central Difference Quotient" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078662}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176793} :activity/id #uuid "bf0ecc39-1fe8-504f-aa46-f80a00074638" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049325 :topic/id #uuid "ec0cc1a2-4f65-5bb2-b5ae-116b4850cf11" :topic/math-academy-id 3671} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177028 :tutorial/content "Recall that Euler's number can be defined as the following special limit:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{m \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{m}\\right)^{m}\n$$\n\nWe can derive an alternative version of this limit by making the substitution\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac 1m\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that if $m\\to \\infty$ then $n \\to 0$. Therefore, applying our substitution, we arrive at the following important result:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+{n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "490157c8-33d8-5f0d-ad6e-f837c839499f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef158b4c-de98-5033-958d-aa50b93d6909" :step/math-academy-id 35058 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176018 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175873 :question/id #uuid "1cc5d11a-c497-5d8f-84b4-9cbef561db28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c8893eb-cc9c-59b1-98c8-ec326a352e28" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049769}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176308}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using the Alternative Limit Definition of Euler's Number"} :step/id #uuid "ac8ee34c-7875-5657-81c3-a58946bcf638" :step/math-academy-id 35060 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176796}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176019 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175874 :question/id #uuid "caad75a3-4ee4-5038-b9e1-5e296d83974a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9366"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dbad5d4-6001-5be5-9ed3-0b959f4d7d92" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176302}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "797a10b6-090b-55e9-8c36-939dfb954adc" :step/math-academy-id 35066 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177029 :tutorial/content "Let's take stock of our two special limit definitions of Euler's number:\n\n- $\\displaystyle e = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{n}$\n- $\\displaystyle e = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{1/n}$\n\nWe can use these results to evaluate more complex limits. For example, suppose we wish to evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{x+3}\\right)^{x+3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $x \\to \\infty$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{x+3}\\right)^{x+3}} = 1^\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^n = e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x+3}=\\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{x + 3} &= \\frac{1}{n} \\\\\nx + 3 &= 2n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)(1 + \\frac{2}{x + 3})^{x + 3} &= lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{2n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)[(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39210b04-2951-5b70-adbb-7a6690671dac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5710 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit That Resembles a Special Limit Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "129e19b0-cbae-5677-bfcb-93c996ee597b" :step/math-academy-id 35067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176022 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175877 :question/id #uuid "63c2e3b3-3418-5f74-8ebc-34af9fd6bf56" :question/math-academy-id "e-9377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9308a83f-ed86-5cbc-9b23-c1fec9fca6a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503687}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using a Variable Substitution: Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "88bbb3ae-6920-5592-b9b2-5833516142b8" :step/math-academy-id 35109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177032 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(1-\\sin{x}\\right)^{3/\\sin{x}}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $x \\to 0$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(1-\\sin{x}\\right)^{3/\\sin{x}} = 1^{\\infty}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{1/n} = e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\sin x)^{3/\\sin x} &= lim_(x → 0)(1 + (-\\sin x))^{3/\\sin x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $-\\sin{x}=n$. Therefore, since $n \\to 0$ as $x \\to 0$, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\sin x)^{3/\\sin x} &= lim_(x → 0)(1 + (-\\sin x))^{3/\\sin x} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{3/(-n)} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)[(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{-3} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{-3} \\\\\n&= e^{-3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{e^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fdccc8f6-9726-5fd5-846c-74c2c5b1ed28" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5717 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit Containing a Minus Sign Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "68a5d41c-4aef-5d14-b412-4042829e22c7" :step/math-academy-id 35113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177035 :tutorial/content "We have shown that Euler's number $e$ is given by the following limit:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+{n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$\n\nLet's use this result to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{2/n}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $n \\to 0$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{2/n}} = 1^\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nHowever, by rewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and then applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{2/n} &= lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{2 \\cdot 1/n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)[(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cde764e2-c036-59de-af59-f13454800e69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5722 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit Using the Alternative Limit Definition of Euler's Number"} :step/id #uuid "1a2ed7d1-2776-5a8c-9da9-bd1e0c7a4cdc" :step/math-academy-id 35122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176794}}] :activity/title "Further Limits Involving the Exponential Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176173}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129905} :activity/id #uuid "70c09bba-cfb5-5906-81b1-66e614d9a1e1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049384 :topic/id #uuid "f08caa4e-a236-53c4-a667-fe4274640c5e" :topic/math-academy-id 2004} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130330 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nbelow.\n\n![](images/cc/cc9593860b0c134d0d99635b37eeac4f16a712a7af9e1c6474fcf1f885e8451e.png)\n\nThe function has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$. There is also a discontinuity in the function at $x=0$, since we cannot draw the points on the curve without taking our pen off the paper to cross the asymptote.\n\nSo, we say that\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nhas a **discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote** at $x=0$. Discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes are also called **infinite discontinuities**.\n\nNotice that here, both one-sided limits are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) =-\\infty\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x \\right)=\\infty\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $y=f(x)$ has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=c$ if either one-sided limit is infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c^-} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to c^+} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\n$$\n\nNote that we only need a single limit to become infinite to conclude that there is an infinite discontinuity at $x=c$." :tutorial/id #uuid "ce83f36f-d7f8-546f-b887-1e6818a75bfb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2595 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "545e237d-8d82-5fad-8893-b53c598fe510" :step/math-academy-id 12112 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128903 :question/id #uuid "001529aa-c97a-5d69-82fb-cd4319a4dd7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3585"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129705}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"} :step/id #uuid "8acbfdc6-b18f-5290-b574-bb7515ced757" :step/math-academy-id 12113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128904 :question/id #uuid "58f2bf5b-04b1-5931-8452-c095b55722ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3586"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048163}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129804}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "bf945475-7921-5714-a012-fd5bfdd13e6b" :step/math-academy-id 12114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128905 :question/id #uuid "26d318a3-0b3f-50be-9802-92b10b83d119" :question/math-academy-id "e-3587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234351}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"} :step/id #uuid "18da17af-7589-58b8-ac89-6eded53c96a2" :step/math-academy-id 12115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128906 :question/id #uuid "83f4e413-e2f4-53dd-9893-fdf8e2df2642" :question/math-academy-id "e-3588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129661}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function"} :step/id #uuid "23a64c25-6876-5b6c-9e24-a82e1cee59ce" :step/math-academy-id 12116}] :activity/title "Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129349}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167750} :activity/id #uuid "aa779b94-f579-5a68-8259-42fd1c8580b5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049506 :topic/id #uuid "fa5d4de4-2fdf-59d1-aea5-7f62da44a307" :topic/math-academy-id 3216} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168125 :tutorial/content "Consider the circle below. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ the slope of the tangent to this circle at any point on the circle? \n\n![](images/4c/4cfdf4a02935f6a729b12a81a5b48d0356e37046fb5dabfdeefc498a68b8ed8d.png)\n\nTo calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$, we start by writing down the equation of the circle. Then, we differentiate the equation of the circle with respect to $x.$\n\nSince our circle is centered at the origin and has a radius of $\\sqrt{5},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = (\\sqrt{5})^2 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^2 + y^2 = 5.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ we differentiate the equation of the circle using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the slope at any point on the circle using our expression for the derivative. \n\nFor example, since the point $P\\big( \\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{3} \\big)$ satisfies the equation $x^2 + y^2 = 5,$ it must lie on the circle. Therefore, the slope of the tangent at this point is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\Bigg|_{( \\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{3})} = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "69315ade-3415-5abd-94e7-90d616fb7002" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5865 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6af5a081-1dac-5c00-aecb-60e48eb5a180" :step/math-academy-id 35885 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166865 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166718 :question/id #uuid "03bebdcd-5908-5c60-b35a-1c8f59593afd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9577"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1331279e-1a5f-5f6f-9104-0c88e1f9f6d0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262781}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Circle"} :step/id #uuid "32f917dc-ab34-51bf-8295-5111957c7a29" :step/math-academy-id 35886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166719 :question/id #uuid "04c9673b-e4e5-508d-acc7-ea0888f2cf87" :question/math-academy-id "e-9578"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e118393-2388-5add-b516-e565abe1e36a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503663}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to an Ellipse"} :step/id #uuid "cd16ea49-d32a-5632-8ad6-d600c7a64959" :step/math-academy-id 35887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166720 :question/id #uuid "58850b39-db80-532d-9f60-808a215da76c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9579"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53a1649d-4279-5b7c-839c-728f333b2c3f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049118}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317244}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "c7dfe24c-f0a2-5e29-a4f6-6589521d6e34" :step/math-academy-id 35888}] :activity/title "Calculating Slopes of Circles, Ellipses, and Parabolas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167000}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106808} :activity/id #uuid "b97eef31-cdcb-509f-a8e2-7468bee3daec" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049713 :topic/id #uuid "0d2ecf90-ccdc-56b7-9726-e90d7669ae2f" :topic/math-academy-id 1362} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105684 :question/id #uuid "ec8ac943-1880-5f2a-81c3-35c136126ec1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8328"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a91e7c54-7ba7-5ea2-99a1-80510e77ac88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106542}] :knowledge-point/title "Basic Derivatives of the Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "287f42f8-ca6c-5b35-9c81-f905b16d1d70" :step/math-academy-id 31128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105685 :question/id #uuid "3dcfc1f2-40ee-54b4-a937-0e101c7238d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8329"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2992a46-063e-589d-a7e9-129e9f97eee6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106540}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions Using the Product Rule"} :step/id #uuid "ae60fdbc-8100-5f74-88dd-dc11838ae028" :step/math-academy-id 31129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105686 :question/id #uuid "1da70111-4e82-5fe4-b4be-08d0cc6f1110" :question/math-academy-id "e-8330"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbeb43f0-d94d-5ee0-b7fb-fbbe0a804cb7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503709}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions Using the Quotient Rule"} :step/id #uuid "2a2959f5-bf52-5984-b73d-213061968d0f" :step/math-academy-id 31130 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105687 :question/id #uuid "192e6748-24e3-5688-87d9-cdf379e24101" :question/math-academy-id "e-8332"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0016464f-27c3-5f1b-a6d3-fe7b5ffb7fbd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106543}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "c2857b6c-e2ae-51ac-8fed-6318597083dc" :step/math-academy-id 31133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107119 :tutorial/content "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives.\n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) &= \\cosh x \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cosh x) &= \\sinh x \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tanh x) &= sech^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x - 2\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) + \\frac{d}{dx}(-2\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) - 2\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\cosh x - 2\\sinh x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "df316238-1ead-50db-80ec-d47656e6acad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5207 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5afd4693-e1ad-5f9f-94cf-364eceabce7d" :step/math-academy-id 32691 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107120 :tutorial/content "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} %\n$$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x})\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} - e^{-x})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x} - e^{-x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + e^{-x}) \\\\\n&= \\cosh x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself." :tutorial/id #uuid "11e38932-d6a4-5649-bbbe-205fb1bad98d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5221 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Sine"} :step/id #uuid "e126b8c5-2d87-5d89-bbf3-8265d50ec279" :step/math-academy-id 32711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106810}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107121 :tutorial/content "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh{x}) = \\sech^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tanh x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{e^{2x} - 1}{e^{2x} + 1}) \\\\\n&= ((e^{2x} - 1)^{′} \\cdot (e^{2x} + 1) - (e^{2x} - 1) \\cdot (e^{2x} + 1)^{′})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((2e^{2x})(e^{2x} + 1) - (e^{2x} - 1)(2e^{2x}))/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x} - 2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2e^{x}}{e^{2x} + 1})^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2}{e^{x} + e^{-x}})^{2} \\\\\n&= sech^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a7389d5-4ba5-5fd7-8edb-b983483e6099" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5223 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "0acbbc47-60c9-5d52-bac4-6df37c07940b" :step/math-academy-id 32713}] :activity/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105995}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101570} :activity/id #uuid "4b7be0a1-49cf-57e8-8286-c52f0151df07" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049825 :topic/id #uuid "16e6877f-e43f-5432-a6fb-d2029db06718" :topic/math-academy-id 1225} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101995 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ k,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1$. So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for\n\n$$\nx \\neq 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 1}{1 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1$, the function is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nf(1)=k\n$$\n\n, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1$, we need to choose\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81a7db1c-98c9-5308-96d8-457b407671db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 548 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d10fc2dc-3be0-5724-8892-7273ab4b7322" :step/math-academy-id 1031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100618 :question/id #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101440}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "080a88b1-8b9c-503a-a435-862f18fa647f" :step/math-academy-id 1032 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100619 :question/id #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" :question/math-academy-id "e-268"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234014}] :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "7867d9d8-2d45-52ba-b25f-cdcdfeac2305" :step/math-academy-id 1035}] :activity/title "Removing Discontinuities From Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101077}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101604} :activity/id #uuid "752a2e8d-dfc4-5210-8dfe-31f383092e9e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050166 :topic/id #uuid "3342477c-42d1-5dd5-8ebd-975b2a6a85a5" :topic/math-academy-id 1204} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102004 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x)$, as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1$, $x=2$, and an inflection point at $x=0.5$.\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)$?\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)$:\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5$. So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)$:\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "732e85fe-aec5-51f6-806c-68e476077346" :tutorial/math-academy-id 641 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "beaa6f10-10cb-5300-9425-d5fce50a2dec" :step/math-academy-id 1224 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100638 :question/id #uuid "c6ce8503-265f-5a02-93f4-ad99390c2734" :question/math-academy-id "e-333"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3433cbc0-fe8c-5b67-a888-4df13b4b68c3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101306}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Smooth Function"} :step/id #uuid "1ebc76df-237a-5efb-96f2-b3585e2b318b" :step/math-academy-id 1225 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100639 :question/id #uuid "68f7ad36-cab7-5ffe-99d9-e720bbf7971a" :question/math-academy-id "e-334"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2ef7a61-79b9-5464-ac2a-3afb7dddd1c2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503654}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Second Derivative of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "fe6a977a-c724-5c50-9d38-b0a7dc613ae3" :step/math-academy-id 1229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100762 :question/id #uuid "98a83d94-0f65-5475-850e-c1abd8836e15" :question/math-academy-id "e-6819"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101304}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "538a0daa-f570-5287-b6c1-2fd7946ab4da" :step/math-academy-id 25278 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100763 :question/id #uuid "0b633f45-0fdb-5d4b-90e0-51c6628ce360" :question/math-academy-id "e-6820"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101483}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function"} :step/id #uuid "826cab52-c133-5e02-bcc7-a04d9190cd1b" :step/math-academy-id 25279 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101606}}] :activity/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Function From the Function's Graph" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101061}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093189} :activity/id #uuid "f7750f7e-ea53-5456-8042-5c68eaecd261" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050209 :topic/id #uuid "368ceda4-e93c-5300-8a14-cc8ed01f5a93" :topic/math-academy-id 972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093189 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093470 :tutorial/content "When solving equations containing hyperbolic functions, one strategy that often works is to rewrite any hyperbolic functions using their definitions. Doing this reduces the problem to solving an exponential equation.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n2\\cosh{x} = 3\n$$\n\nTo find the real solutions to this equation, we first recall that the definition of $\\cosh{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\cosh{x} = \\dfrac12(e^x + e^{-x})\n$$\n\nSubstituting this definition into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac12(e^x+e^{-x}) \\right) = 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\ne^x + e^{-x} = 3\n$$\n\nWe can eliminate the $e^{-x}$ term by multiplying both sides of the equation by $e^x$ and simplifying by bringing all terms to one side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x} \\cdot (e^{x} + e^{-x}) &= e^{x} \\cdot 3 \\\\\ne^{x} \\cdot e^{x} + e^{x} \\cdot e^{-x} &= 3e^{x} \\\\\ne^{2x} + 1 &= 3e^{x} \\\\\ne^{2x} - 3e^{x} + 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is a quadratic equation in $e^x$. To see this, note that we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n\\left(e^{x}\\right)^2 - 3e^x + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nand if we let $y=e^x$, we get\n\n$$\ny^2 - 3y + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the quadratic formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (- (-3) ± \\sqrt{(-3)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1})/(2 \\cdot 1) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 ± \\sqrt{9 - 4}}{2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 ± \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\\ne^{x} &= \\frac{3 ± \\sqrt{5}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the natural logarithm of both sides, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\ln{\\left(\\dfrac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nSince we want real solutions only, we require that the argument of the logarithm is positive. However, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} > 0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\dfrac{3 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} > 0\n$$\n\nthis means that *both* of our solutions are valid.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solutions to our equation are\n\n$$\nx=\\ln{\\left(\\dfrac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nWhen solving these equations, we often skip the intermediate step of setting $y=e^x$. However, you can continue to do this if you wish." :tutorial/id #uuid "784cb533-655f-5e6e-bee6-3abed97bd388" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6092 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7c211b14-dc28-5522-a231-ab736cca558a" :step/math-academy-id 37044 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093192}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093190 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092307 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092154 :question/id #uuid "939d725b-cda6-539e-a4a4-ecd426edd6df" :question/math-academy-id "e-9849"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a06fde5-14f9-5285-b92d-f08820423ff8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092549}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Containing a Hyperbolic Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "1c405839-3218-5def-b570-d4ac19be3800" :step/math-academy-id 37045 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092308 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092155 :question/id #uuid "7c4bceb1-413e-530e-8caa-beb9490cdb42" :question/math-academy-id "e-9850"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e30c14-d6f7-5585-bad2-4ddff6ed297f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092933}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Containing Multiple Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "7e687e18-8da5-57ef-9742-c99cfda0e04a" :step/math-academy-id 37046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092309 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092156 :question/id #uuid "3539387d-eee8-5d50-a820-79c135d435f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9855"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3597822-16fc-5be4-af47-7480ab9a46fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050649}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503669}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Containing a Hyperbolic Sine or Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "f367898d-0713-517c-8362-b13a3564dc65" :step/math-academy-id 37074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093190}}] :activity/title "Solving Equations Containing Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092454}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065317} :activity/id #uuid "bdb82b74-3fdc-5083-a106-20c7dc1287f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411 :topic/id #uuid "46d83f8d-b8f7-5858-99d2-3e75f2b9bd8c" :topic/math-academy-id 287} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065384 :tutorial/content "Suppose we're told that the radius of a circle is **growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second**, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27d0543834333202411a9700401b7e33329a255c7103c9f2ebe394d8c1cf694b.png)\n\nIf the radius of the circle is growing, then its area is also increasing. How do we calculate the **rate at which the area is increasing**?\n\nThe rate at which the area is changing can be expressed using a derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is the area of the circle, measured in $\\textrm{cm}^2,$ and $t$ is the time in seconds. \n\nWe know that the area is related to the radius $r$ as\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the radius of a circle is growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt} = 2 \\, \\textrm{cm}.$ Substituting this information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot 2\\pi r=4\\pi r\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd, that's it, we're done! \n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $r$ at that moment into the above. So, for example, if we want to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at the moment when $r=6\\,\\textrm{cm},$ we plug this into the above and get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4\\pi \\cdot 6 = 24\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6925e269-127a-5cf1-8116-aa809d1cf42f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2f58c76e-8d14-575a-b5de-62cc2003eb38" :step/math-academy-id 906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064379 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064023 :question/id #uuid "7aceecc7-33ca-5a7e-a4aa-cf1a313785fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-225"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395797}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "a12978db-32b6-52fc-a426-abfda5e434ae" :step/math-academy-id 907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064380 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064024 :question/id #uuid "fba09533-20f9-5e4c-9786-cfbe7b179512" :question/math-academy-id "e-226"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050089}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371056}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle"} :step/id #uuid "9c671f5f-a9e8-5694-8457-ab3f9df8b239" :step/math-academy-id 910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064381 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064025 :question/id #uuid "87c5af76-7652-525a-aeb2-d419bab613e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-227"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050966}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395790}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Sphere: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "318a88e0-e4a9-5b31-a8c3-9cb7712ff53b" :step/math-academy-id 913}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates With Circles and Spheres" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064393}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101614} :activity/id #uuid "1e401592-79bc-5a8c-b040-fe705dfe9110" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050544 :topic/id #uuid "525bb99e-c082-5a7b-a26b-369998ff4ac8" :topic/math-academy-id 1207} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102007 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to sketch a possible graph of a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- $f'(3)=0$\n- if $x \\in (-\\infty,3)$ then $f'(x)> 0$\n- if $x \\in (3,+\\infty)$ then $f'(x)<0$\n- $f''(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n\nWe summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f''$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow\\:\\:\\frown$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow\\:\\:\\frown$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph:\n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=3$, where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n- Since $x=3$ is a local maximum, the graph must go down to the left and to the right of that point.\n- Finally, the graph must be concave down everywhere.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7c6d8412e9c657e8e4a148e40ca0b8a073735d3b88681eec6a79f763471aaf4f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f1b863e-e172-5e84-8f8d-1ea21025605d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 655 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a979657-a144-5dbc-997b-c1dd2371aada" :step/math-academy-id 1253 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100642 :question/id #uuid "744696c5-253d-5254-b7cc-d5dbcac566b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-341"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa143f7f-c07f-55dd-8907-d8b01ef8f418" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503658}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given Some Derivative Properties"} :step/id #uuid "f59c1bb5-ff3b-5994-8b51-510587d9b7d0" :step/math-academy-id 1254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100643 :question/id #uuid "f6454a91-9fbf-55b3-9a93-873e74e66c1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-342"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1edb2bb1-a64a-5520-b6e2-4b3ff1fa4cf2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050120}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101472}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of a Smooth Function With One Critical Point Given Some Derivative Properties"} :step/id #uuid "049dcac4-b6ea-586e-a449-913e4b288153" :step/math-academy-id 1257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100941 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100773 :question/id #uuid "40423e14-da7f-587f-9206-bf179767700e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7222"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3859c754-da12-5f90-a97f-6f1c1f0b3629" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101471}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Smooth Function Given Some Derivative Properties"} :step/id #uuid "21d8a180-a349-5a05-bcdf-9edbed0ff759" :step/math-academy-id 26716 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101615}}] :activity/title "Sketching a Function Given Some Derivative Properties" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101063}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079268} :activity/id #uuid "128934d0-aa66-5625-aab3-6d772fa10846" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050643 :topic/id #uuid "5ba94c26-75d7-5806-9fad-73f65947835b" :topic/math-academy-id 637} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079652 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to calculate the limit\n\n$$\nL=\\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln(2+h) -\\ln(2)}{h}\n$$\n\nThe key here is to notice that the limit looks very similar to the instantaneous rate of change formula,\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nfor the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\ln x\n$$\n\nat $a=2$.\n\nLet's substitute\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\ln x\n$$\n\nand $a=2$ into the above, just to be sure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= lim_(h → 0)(f(2 + h) - f(2))/(h) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\ln (2 + h) - \\ln (2))/(h) \\\\\n&= L\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L = f'(2)$. So instead of working out that difficult limit, we only need to work out $f'(2)$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\frac{1}{x}, \\qquad f'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that\n\n$$\nL=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "db8ca187-38da-5852-8df8-cd1cb0f754cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2631 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3b7e08e0-d936-5e59-996f-679f185c3562" :step/math-academy-id 12352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078279 :question/id #uuid "5f4f75ce-d8df-5970-bffe-99beb9d9aac9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c089a-9506-5a0e-83ae-48efa8fd314c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234076}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Instantaneous Rate of Change Formula"} :step/id #uuid "cca4127e-8b00-5649-abdf-3f0477315c35" :step/math-academy-id 12353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079653 :tutorial/content "We can apply the same method for calculating limits that are similar to the equivalent definition of the derivative,\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\n$$\n\nIt is important to choose the correct form for each limit. If the numerator of the limit looks like $f(a+h) -f(a)$ for some $a$ and the denominator looks like $h$, then use the form\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nBut if the numerator looks like $f(x)-f(a)$ and the denominator looks like $x-a$ for some $a$, then use the form\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "88b647b7-8adf-517b-848b-5323c6565ced" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2632 :tutorial/title "Recognizing the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative in Limits"} :step/id #uuid "a1fec940-4ec4-58c8-8553-9ddb7fd70bff" :step/math-academy-id 12354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078280 :question/id #uuid "3b5966f8-c23f-5ef1-8e5c-7862a020402d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3651"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b613bf7-a641-547f-8487-d6af0da0f724" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079003}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "4bbfd52a-c53b-5698-a9e3-0f3cadd9059f" :step/math-academy-id 12355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078409 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078281 :question/id #uuid "4a42aea1-4927-5d6e-af94-d43b834e295a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3652"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20df0d0d-a94e-55e6-8d19-25a566ab794d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079005}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits Equivalent to a Derivative Using a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "6d9b76a5-30f4-5ef3-8887-46953ed35bc3" :step/math-academy-id 12356}] :activity/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078660}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185041} :activity/id #uuid "5c37473f-3f15-50ad-88ea-ddb970802b55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050756 :topic/id #uuid "642d830f-d131-58c7-ae56-060749a40776" :topic/math-academy-id 3811} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185359 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bc1975-006b-5be4-ad5c-88888abb00d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6599 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "003ba853-1e82-584c-b1f2-becf3ff9c30c" :step/math-academy-id 39810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184238 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183921 :question/id #uuid "563087c2-8d87-5b95-833d-f9892d9276a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050071}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314754}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "e4ed7c3e-7162-558c-a918-39f63652e0c0" :step/math-academy-id 39811 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183922 :question/id #uuid "c1ba0b9f-78d6-5791-8816-b71da089e57f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050085}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317370}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "9666a609-3291-5b2c-a5f1-4c905924ddd1" :step/math-academy-id 39814 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185360 :tutorial/content "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$ \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "24cdcba9-d6c5-5719-a086-34fe417cc42f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6600 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Function at Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "66882814-9d8d-500a-b199-d60870f60cec" :step/math-academy-id 39815 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183923 :question/id #uuid "7ce46ca9-cfff-5e8b-89b9-a2a7b34921fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317512}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"} :step/id #uuid "f8205256-4d92-59b5-babd-ec4ae58b06b8" :step/math-academy-id 39823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185362 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "926b00b5-d0b1-559a-9b04-c0c0636824d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6741 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "338537ec-9b29-534a-bc92-7f0375a54a6d" :step/math-academy-id 40843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185363 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eea2d2ac-ea9c-551a-91c6-4ee85ab05907" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6742 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "13c5d606-b95e-5a64-ba04-2a68fbf50e14" :step/math-academy-id 40844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185051}}] :activity/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184398}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106748} :activity/id #uuid "58252d2b-905f-529d-99aa-1e8c7d226207" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050785 :topic/id #uuid "66e72e13-4959-5704-8bb3-c0ffea916dd8" :topic/math-academy-id 1384} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107031 :tutorial/content "Rational functions have infinite limits when the $x$-value approaches vertical asymptotes. To find the infinite limits of a rational function $f(x)$, we first find the vertical asymptotes.\n\nFor example, consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-2}\n$$\n\nThis rational function has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.\n\nTo find the one-sided limits at the asymptote $x=2$, we check the sign of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and the right.\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $1.9$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-10$ | $-100$ | $-1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the right:\n\n| $x$ | $2.1$ | $2.01$ | $2.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $100$ | $1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nThese one-sided limits match up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/24/248e651214f9339d5e47f09f3eecd3387897eda525d99845258d4466025f16ac.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5a2dd1f-90e1-5bcc-a854-1debabaffe1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1338 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5567f868-361e-566c-80a1-f886af15f9ca" :step/math-academy-id 2651 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105673 :question/id #uuid "065919d1-7e77-5258-ba32-50b4a2a62942" :question/math-academy-id "e-775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2838ed19-abed-560e-8721-09afa1d696ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106441}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Right-Sided Limit of a Rational Function at a Vertical Asymptote"} :step/id #uuid "3e10f3c1-5b3b-5adb-9fd0-0a1e4d8f3f23" :step/math-academy-id 2652 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105674 :question/id #uuid "af0efa71-6027-5026-bd98-f7af0631ccde" :question/math-academy-id "e-776"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92b5db1f-0fb2-50af-a851-045cc8cd1d9c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106443}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Left-Sided Limit of a Rational Function at a Vertical Asymptote"} :step/id #uuid "bcefc791-a469-5133-b984-bf5f1294532f" :step/math-academy-id 2654 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105675 :question/id #uuid "779e33dc-0411-5cef-a505-df1e5a5c070e" :question/math-academy-id "e-777"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a13f946-b2fa-5da3-a09d-6a8086102b94" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106451}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Limits of a Given Rational Function"} :step/id #uuid "ade971db-c5ab-5cf8-bede-d4db99d2b5f8" :step/math-academy-id 2656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105678 :question/id #uuid "4228f47a-3a9b-54e4-82a6-b72837039d70" :question/math-academy-id "e-779"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "969d32c6-b343-5e1f-9521-94ff346645b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050323}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233097}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Limits of a Given Function when Simplification is Required"} :step/id #uuid "c44388fb-c586-558c-b35f-1e1ef27bb6fd" :step/math-academy-id 2660}] :activity/title "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106008}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101583} :activity/id #uuid "e216932a-7285-534e-b666-8b8934782d02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050821 :topic/id #uuid "694fcacb-2468-51b2-bf00-fd3c25bc6cba" :topic/math-academy-id 1210} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101997 :tutorial/content "The function $f(x)$ is differentiable on an open interval containing $x=2$. The values of $f$ and $f'$ are given in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nLet's find\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{f(x)}{x^3-8}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we try to substitute $x=2$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8) &= \\left.(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8)\\right|_{x = 2} \\\\\n&= (f(2))/(2^{3} - 8) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{8 - 8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8) &= lim_(x → 2)((f(x))^{′})/((x^{3} - 8)^{′}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2$ again, and this time, we are able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2}) &= \\left.(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2})\\right|_{x = 2} \\\\\n&= (f^{′}(2))/(3 \\cdot 2^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3 \\cdot 2^{2} - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{f(x)}{x^3-8}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c8d2f073-f126-5950-9555-61f8a6dd159a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 599 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e4ea661d-9e67-59ef-ae74-3ef55f82ab3a" :step/math-academy-id 1137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100623 :question/id #uuid "127247a2-3209-51aa-acb1-eceeaa0dec33" :question/math-academy-id "e-304"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c185115e-e3ce-5503-ab5f-a3d0b1a49cfd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051036}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101224}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "06be6b30-b825-5dbe-9087-f089e5a3c396" :step/math-academy-id 1138 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100624 :question/id #uuid "89a99552-00c5-5850-a567-1b338ab5e16b" :question/math-academy-id "e-305"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e43a1e-2073-593b-9b55-4f9c25c356a8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234323}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"} :step/id #uuid "97a0a8f1-3fcd-5681-9176-f997a3d0c81b" :step/math-academy-id 1142}] :activity/title "L'Hopital's Rule Applied to Tables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101065}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101634} :activity/id #uuid "9177baa9-434a-5663-a141-bf8266321264" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050853 :topic/id #uuid "6c00fb56-453a-5851-b57f-240fff460160" :topic/math-academy-id 1217} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102013 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the general strategy for optimization problems:\n\n1. Draw a diagram of the situation, introducing variables where necessary.\n2. Write down the equation of the quantity that you're trying to maximize or minimize. This is called the **objective function**, and it might include more than one variable.\n3. Write down the constraint equation.\n4. Use the constraint equation to write the objective function in terms of a single variable only.\n5. Differentiate the objective function, set it equal to zero, and solve for the stationary points.\n6. Test each stationary point using the second derivative test. Or you can use the first derivative test if it's easier.\n\nWe can use differentiation to solve optimization problems involving inscribed shapes. Let's find out how." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b58b0ee-ece2-52dc-8624-cdfc8dcf2fcb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 679 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e426b8b7-eb67-57e7-9bc8-f65a27e3c1e4" :step/math-academy-id 1307 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101635 :step/content {:db/id #